<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagera Region</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagera Region is an administrative region of north-western Tanzania, bordering Uganda, Rwanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> to the east and its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a>. Named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a>, it is the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haya-people">Haya people</a>, whose small kingdoms such as Kyamutwara, Kiziba and Kianja survived under German and British rule until chieftaincies were abolished in 1963. Uganda's invasion of the region in 1978 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-tanzania-war">Uganda–Tanzania War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagera-region.jpg" alt="Kagera Region" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Macabe5387 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mater_Misericordiae_Church_-_Bukoba.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kagera Region covers 35,686 square kilometres, of which 27 percent is water, including parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, Lake Ikimba, Burigi and Ngono, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a>. The river forms the region's border with Rwanda and gives it its name. Hills run parallel to the lake shore from north to south. Rainfall is heaviest along the shore, about 2000 mm a year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a>, and falls to about 500 mm in the west. The region lies on the Kivu Rift, the western branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, and a magnitude 5.9 earthquake struck it on 10 September 2016, killing 17 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For about five centuries Kagera was home to nine kingdoms with highly hierarchical societies, among them Karagwe, Misenye, Bugabo, Ihangiro, Bukara and Biharamulo. Their kings, called omukama, lived in elaborate palaces and were respected as the direct link to the gods of their kingdoms. Women held special respect in traditional ritual. When a king died, a sister chosen from among his sisters took part in the crowning of the next king as the sister to the nation, and a head of a family had a female counterpart to share the authority of his inheritor.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> colonised Tanzania in 1885 and favoured the Haya, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a> and Muleba districts. The local kings nevertheless kept their power, and some, such as Kahigi, gave up their territories by collaborating with the Germans. In 1897 and 1898 the local medical officer, Dr M. Zupitza, met a plague outbreak in Kiziba and, working with Dr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-koch">Robert Koch</a>, confirmed that it had the same cause as the outbreak in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. After authority passed to the British following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, Lutheran missionaries began work in the region, and other Christian denominations followed.</p><p>In the colonial period the area was called West Lake and belonged to Lake Province. After independence it became the West Lake Region, and it took the name Kagera after the war that began with Uganda's attempted annexation of the region. President Nyerere regarded the kingdoms as detrimental to national unity, and they ended after independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a> is the regional capital, about 1,500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>. Kagera is one of Tanzania's 31 regions, and its 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> population was 2,989,299. Agriculture is the main economic activity, and the region is one of the country's largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> producers. Its protected areas include Burigi-Chato, Rumanyika-Karagwe, Ibanda-Kyerwa and Rubondo Island national parks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kagera Region” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bugabo emerged around 1800 from the division of the Kingdom of Kyamutwara in the <b>Kagera region</b> of north-western Tanzania, one of several small Haya states created by succession disputes among royal lines.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bugabo">Kingdom of Bugabo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ihangiro emerged around the 17th century in the <b>Kagera region</b> of north-western Tanzania, one of several small kingdoms of the Haya people.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ihangiro">Kingdom of Ihangiro</a></li><li><blockquote>Kianja was formed around 1800 when succession disputes divided the Kingdom of Kyamutwara in the <b>Kagera region</b> west of Lake Victoria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kianja">Kingdom of Kianja</a></li><li><blockquote>Kyamutwara arose in the 17th century in the <b>Kagera region</b>, near today's Bukoba, among the Haya people.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kyamutwara">Kingdom of Kyamutwara</a></li></ul></section>
